{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310670,"nonce":"KS1ezM8YZkXxOxEPCCmnw4gjuII=","title":"OPEN — Std.parseInt failure returns the raw i64::MIN sentinel; `== null` is FALSE, the null branch is not taken, and it prints ...","message":"**REPRO** (`/tmp/probe/O2.hx`), `var bad = Std.parseInt(\"zz\")`:\n\n| expression | got | want |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bad == null` | `false` | `true` |\n| `bad != null` | `true` | `false` |\n| `\"\" + bad` | `-9223372036854775808` | `null` |\n| `if (bad == null)` | takes the ELSE branch | takes the null branch |\n\n`Std.parseInt(\"42\")` and its `!= null` are correct, so only the FAILURE path\nis wrong.\n\n**MECHANISM.** `haxe_std_parse_int` (runtime/src/type_system.rs:2323) returns\n`i64::MIN` and its own comment calls it \"Sentinel for null\". **Nothing\nconsumes that sentinel** — no comparison, branch or stringify site translates\nit back to null, so it escapes as an ordinary integer.\n\n**HALF FIXED d3ff6460, AND THE CHOICE IS SETTLED: THE BOX WINS.** It is the\nonly representation convertible to BOTH uses — `== null` reads its tag,\narithmetic goes through the unboxing the Optional path already does. The\ncomparison half landed: `haxe_dynamic_is_null` + a null-compare branch, which\nalso fixed `m.get(missing) == null` (previously FALSE, so the null branch was\nnever taken). **Only `Null\u003cprimitive\u003e` may take it** — `Null\u003cSomeClass\u003e` IS the\nobject pointer with no header, and reading one as a box made\n`test_null_receiver_method` skip the call it guards.\n\n**THE parseInt HALF WAS TRIED AND REVERTED (2026-09-10).** Mapping it to a\nboxed `haxe_std_parse_int_boxed` fixed `== null`, the branch and stringify —\nbut conformance went +1/-1 (LOST Issue2874: `Std.parseInt` through\n`a.map(f)` leaked the box as `1 != 40972467792`) and the Haxe suite went to\n1 FAILED. The box escapes wherever a closure or generic carries it into an\nInt context without the unboxing. **Finish that unboxing FIRST, then re-map\nparseInt** — the mapping change itself is one line.\n\n**WHY IT WAS NOT A ONE-LINE FIX.** `Null\u003cInt\u003e` has TWO representations in this\ncompiler: this sentinel, and the BOXED DynamicValue that `Map.get` and an\ninferred `Null\u003cInt\u003e` return (see\n[[bugs_erased_null_t_box_at_type_param_boundary]]). Picking one is the real\nwork:\n- Boxing parseInt's result (a MIR wrapper returning a null box or an Int box,\n  the `String_indexOf`/`String_indexOf_2` arity idiom from\n  [[bugs_substring_one_arg_defaults_end_to_zero]]) makes `== null` and\n  stringify correct through the existing Dynamic machinery, but changes the\n  return representation for every existing caller — `Std.parseInt(s) + 1`\n  then needs the unboxing path.\n- Keeping the sentinel means teaching every consumer about it. A Haxe `Int` is\n  32-bit, so `i64::MIN` can never be a legitimate result and the test is safe\n  — but it has to be added at each comparison, branch and string conversion,\n  and it would NOT cover the boxed `Null\u003cInt\u003e` values that arrive from\n  elsewhere.\n\nMEASURE EITHER CHOICE ON THE CORPUS: parseInt is common, so a representation\nchange can move many rows in either direction.\n\n**Everything around it is correct** — probed in the same battery (`/tmp/probe/O1.hx`,\n18 checks): override/virtual/interface dispatch, inherited fields and methods,\n`super()`, `Std.isOfType`, abstract `@:op(A + B)`, an abstract's `toString`\nthrough `Std.string`, enum abstract values and methods, `parseFloat`,\n`Std.int(7/2)`, `7 % 3`, `-7 % 3`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_parseint_null_sentinel_never_translated` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}